---
title: Benchmarks
description: Throughput and latency benchmarks for QUIC and WebSocket transports, including degraded network performance with packet loss and latency.
order: 5
---

# Benchmarks

These benchmarks measure the cost of the tunnel itself and how each transport holds up when the network gets rough. The goal is to give you enough data to pick the right transport for the job.

All results use `iperf3` pushing traffic through a full end-to-end tunnel with encryption active. Entry and exit run as QEMU microVMs on the same host (2 vCPUs each, KVM, Linux x86_64). Degraded scenarios use `tc netem` on the entry VM's ethernet interface to simulate real-world loss and latency. Each run is 5 seconds. To reproduce: `just bench benchmark` from the repo root.

## Clean Network

No packet loss or added latency. This isolates the cost of encryption, framing, and multiplexing.

### Single Stream

| Metric | QUIC | WebSocket |
|--------|------|-----------|
| TCP upstream (to exit) | 830 Mbps | 900 Mbps |
| TCP downstream (to entry) | 300 Mbps | 330 Mbps |
| UDP | 5.3 Gbps | 4.5 Gbps |
| Latency (RTT) | 0.7 ms | 1.1 ms |

Downstream is lower than upstream because the entry-side userspace TCP stack must generate TCP segments from tunnel data. UDP throughput is high because there is no congestion window or retransmission overhead on the inner flow. QUIC has 35% lower latency.

### Parallel Streams

| Streams | QUIC | WebSocket |
|---------|------|-----------|
| 4 | - | - |
| 8 | - | - |
| 32 | 794 Mbps | 932 Mbps |
| 64 | 744 Mbps | 896 Mbps |
| 128 | 578 Mbps | 815 Mbps |

On a clean network, WebSocket is faster. It benefits from the OS kernel's highly optimised TCP stack. No surprises here. But clean networks are not where tunnels get tested.

## Degraded Network

This is where transport choice actually matters. Pen test tunnels run over hotel wifi, mobile tethering, intercontinental VPNs, and congested corporate networks. These links have packet loss and latency. The question is: which transport survives?

### Loss + Latency (80 ms RTT)

40 ms one-way delay (80 ms round-trip, typical cross-country link). Loss applied on the entry VM's egress. Throughput in Mbps.

| Loss | p32 QUIC | p32 WS | p64 QUIC | p64 WS | p128 QUIC | p128 WS |
|------|----------|--------|----------|--------|-----------|---------|
| 0.1% | 110 | 66 | 103 | 71 | 150 | 112 |
| 0.5% | 54 | 48 | 68 | 66 | 92 | 0 |
| 1% | 49 | 45 | 69 | 68 | 95 | 0 |
| 2% | 52 | 46 | 67 | 62 | 91 | 0 |
| 3% | 51 | 43 | 66 | 66 | 93 | 0 |
| 5% | 49 | 46 | 63 | 0 | 90 | 0 |

**WebSocket collapses as stream count increases.** At 128 parallel connections, WebSocket produces zero throughput at just 0.5% loss. QUIC maintains 90+ Mbps even at 5% loss. At 64 streams, WebSocket dies at 5% loss. At 32 streams, WebSocket survives but is consistently slower.

This matters because real pen test workloads open many connections simultaneously: nmap scanning, multiple RDP sessions, parallel exploitation, or file transfers alongside interactive shells.

### Latency Sweep (1% Loss)

Fixed 1% packet loss, varying one-way delay. This shows how latency amplifies the damage from loss. Throughput in Mbps.

| Delay (one-way) | p32 QUIC | p32 WS | p64 QUIC | p64 WS | p128 QUIC | p128 WS |
|------------------|----------|--------|----------|--------|-----------|---------|
| 1 ms | 67 | 107 | 95 | 69 | 165 | 105 |
| 10 ms | 62 | 56 | 72 | 68 | 106 | 107 |
| 20 ms | 54 | 45 | 68 | 66 | 99 | 0 |
| 40 ms | 54 | 48 | 67 | 67 | 95 | 0 |
| 80 ms | 52 | 43 | 66 | 64 | 97 | 0 |
| 150 ms | 52 | 38 | 60 | 0 | 90 | 0 |

At 128 streams with 1% loss, WebSocket dies above 10 ms one-way delay (20 ms RTT). At 64 streams, WebSocket holds until 150 ms. At 32 streams, WebSocket degrades but survives.

QUIC throughput scales with stream count under adversity: more streams means more independent recovery paths. WebSocket throughput degrades with stream count because all streams share a single TCP connection and a single lost packet stalls everything.

### Latency Only (No Loss)

Pure delay, no packet loss. This isolates the effect of distance. Throughput in Mbps.

| Delay (one-way) | p32 QUIC | p32 WS | p64 QUIC | p64 WS | p128 QUIC | p128 WS |
|------------------|----------|--------|----------|--------|-----------|---------|
| 1 ms | 394 | 653 | 345 | 1039 | 379 | 687 |
| 10 ms | 142 | 429 | 149 | 449 | 175 | 447 |
| 40 ms | 146 | 110 | 163 | 101 | 176 | 155 |
| 80 ms | 135 | 95 | 150 | 70 | 171 | 112 |
| 150 ms | 134 | 59 | 145 | 68 | 195 | 108 |

Without loss, WebSocket wins at low latency. But above 40 ms one-way delay (~80 ms RTT, a typical cross-country hop), QUIC takes over and the gap widens with distance. At 150 ms one-way (intercontinental), QUIC is 1.8-2.3x faster depending on stream count.

## Why QUIC Survives

WebSocket multiplexes all tunnel connections over a single TCP connection. When a packet is lost, TCP's head-of-line blocking stalls every stream until that packet is retransmitted. Each retransmission costs a full round-trip. More streams means more chances for any one packet loss to stall everything.

QUIC multiplexes over independent streams within the same connection. A lost packet only stalls the affected stream. The other streams continue uninterrupted. More streams means more independent recovery, which is why QUIC throughput actually increases with stream count under loss.

## Which Transport Should I Use?

**Use QUIC (the default)** for most pen test scenarios. It handles unpredictable networks, high latency, and packet loss without collapsing. This covers remote engagements, cloud-hosted targets, mobile tethering, hotel wifi, and anything over the internet.

**Use WebSocket** when you need to traverse an HTTP proxy, blend in with HTTPS traffic for DPI evasion, or front through a CDN. Also appropriate on reliable low-latency networks (same data centre, local LAN) where its raw throughput advantage matters and loss is negligible.

If you are unsure, use QUIC. You can always switch transports by changing the address suffix (`/udp` or `/tcp`) without reconfiguring anything else.
